{"id":"n-20261028-5r7mot","category":"gaming","display_title":"Doom Creators Debate History","display_title_he":"יוצרי דום מתווכחים על ההיסטוריה","short_summary":"Two original creators of the 1993 classic video game Doom recently engaged in a public disagreement on social media regarding the early development history of its groundbreaking DeathMatch multiplayer mode.","english_level":"C1","words":420,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"IGN","domain":"ign.com","url":"https://www.ign.com/articles/two-creators-of-the-original-doom-are-arguing-about-its-history-on-social-media","title":"Two Creators of the Original Doom Are Arguing About its History on Social Media","published_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-28T07:01:58.476Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["The iconic 1993 first-person shooter Doom holds an untouchable and inarguable status in video game history. However, specific details regarding its creation have recently become the subject of an online dispute on social media platform X, formerly known as Twitter. Rather than enthusiastic fans, the debate involves two key figures who actually helped build the legendary title: Doom co-creator John Romero and former level designer Sandy Petersen.","The public disagreement began when Petersen published a detailed thread titled \\'How Doom changed the world,\\' reflecting on his career and his time working at id Software. He recalled his prior experience contributing to a 1990 real-time strategy game called Command HQ, noting that designer Danielle Bunten Berry had anticipated robust modem play, only for the title to commercialize poorly with roughly forty thousand sales. According to Petersen, this past failure shaped team expectations at id Software, where staff supposedly agreed that very few people would ever engage in head-to-head multiplayer because no robust prior games had succeeded in that space. Nevertheless, Petersen stated that the team kept the feature because they personally enjoyed playing Deathmatch against one another.","John Romero quickly challenged this narrative online, posting a polite yet firm correction. Romero pointed out that id Software had issued an official press release on January first, nineteen ninety-three, fully eight months prior to Petersen joining the company. This document explicitly predicted that multiplayer capability inside Doom would become the primary cause of diminished workplace productivity worldwide. Romero argued that the team held absolute confidence in the massive potential of Deathmatch from the very beginning and required no convincing. Furthermore, Romero expressed concern that Petersen\\'s statements were designed to trigger replies, reposts, and outrage discussion to increase followers and subscriptions.","Following Romero\\'s public rebuttal, Petersen defended his perspective by explaining that his recollection was based on a direct conversation he had with Romero after joining the studio, during which Romero allegedly agreed that the feature might not be exceptionally significant. Petersen expressed frustration, questioning Romero\\'s memory and accusing him of gaslighting. In response, Romero maintained his stance, asserting that his own recollection and that of other team members remained entirely clear regarding their early aspirations.","Regardless of these conflicting pre-release memories, Deathmatch undeniably revolutionized the gaming industry once Doom officially shipped in December nineteen ninety-three. Every modern multiplayer first-person shooter traces some portion of its architectural and design lineage back to that seminal release. Meanwhile, the franchise continues to evolve through modern iterations developed by the current team at id Software."],"facts":[{"fact":"Doom co-creator John Romero and level designer Sandy Petersen publicly debated the history of Doom's DeathMatch multiplayer mode on X.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Sandy Petersen claimed that the id Software team originally believed nobody would play Deathmatch due to the poor commercial performance of the 1990 game Command HQ.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"John Romero disputed Petersen's recollection, pointing to an official January 1, 1993 press release predicting that multiplayer would be a massive productivity hindrance.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Doom was released in December 1993, establishing a foundational lineage for all subsequent multiplayer first-person shooters.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"iconic","lemma":"iconic","pos":"adj","he":"איקוני","name":false},{"text":"first-person shooter","lemma":"first-person shooter","pos":"n","he":"משחק יריות בגוף ראשון","name":false},{"text":"Doom","lemma":"Doom","pos":"n","he":"דום (שם משחק)","name":true},{"text":"holds","lemma":"hold","pos":"v","he":"מחזיק / שומר על","name":false},{"text":"untouchable","lemma":"untouchable","pos":"adj","he":"בלתי נגיע / חסין","name":false},{"text":"inarguable","lemma":"inarguable","pos":"adj","he":"שאינו נתון לויכוח","name":false},{"text":"status","lemma":"status","pos":"n","he":"מעמד","name":false},{"text":"video game","lemma":"video game","pos":"n","he":"משחק וידאו","name":false},{"text":"history","lemma":"history","pos":"n","he":"היסטוריה","name":false},{"text":"specific","lemma":"specific","pos":"adj","he":"ספציפי","name":false},{"text":"details","lemma":"detail","pos":"n","he":"פרטים","name":false},{"text":"regarding","lemma":"regard","pos":"prep","he":"בנוגע ל-","name":false},{"text":"creation","lemma":"creation","pos":"n","he":"יצירה","name":false},{"text":"recently","lemma":"recently","pos":"adv","he":"לאחרונה","name":false},{"text":"become","lemma":"become","pos":"v","he":"להפוך ל-","name":false},{"text":"subject","lemma":"subject","pos":"n","he":"נושא","name":false},{"text":"online","lemma":"online","pos":"adj","he":"מקוון","name":false},{"text":